---
intent: >
  Refactor the runtime brain tool to the four-state contract: collapse the
  status union from 5 to 4 by removing `invalid-path`, delete the
  INVALID_PATH_PROSE constant, update NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE to point at
  `.aide/config/brain.aide`, retarget the defensive fallback (parse-non-ok
  despite buildBrainState ok) to NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE, and rewrite the tests
  so they cover the 4 status branches plus the new fallback target while
  asserting the response shape never carries `name`, `backend`, or
  `connector`.
---

## Project Structure

```
src/tools/brain/
├── .aide                ← [existing, aligned] intent spec for the runtime brain tool
├── plan.aide            ← [this file]
├── todo.aide            ← [existing, untouched] alignment cycle log
├── index.ts             ← [edit] orchestrator: brain(root: string): Promise<BrainToolResult>
│                          imports buildBrainState (4-state union) and parseBrainAide
│                          three top-of-file remediation prose constants:
│                            NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE (path now `.aide/config/brain.aide`)
│                            NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE  (unchanged text)
│                            MCP_DRIFT_PROSE     (unchanged text)
│                          INVALID_PATH_PROSE: DELETED
│                          branches: no-brain-aide | no-mcp-entry | mcp-drift | ok
│                          ok branch: parseBrainAide → return { status: "ok", instructions: prose }
│                          defensive fallback (parse non-ok despite ok state):
│                            return { status: "no-brain-aide", instructions: NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE }
│                          response shape: { status, instructions } only — never name/backend/connector
└── index.test.ts        ← [rewrite] 4-state branch tests + defensive fallback to no-brain-aide
                           verifies response shape has no `name`, no `backend`, no `connector`
                           verifies verbatim-prose invariant on ok branch
                           verifies instructions never empty on every branch + fallback
                           drops every reference to `invalid-path` and INVALID_PATH_PROSE
```

This plan is module-scoped. The `BrainToolResult` type lives in
`src/types/index.ts` (outside this scope) and the four-state `BrainState`
lives in the same shared types module. Both type changes are listed under
Prerequisites.

## Prerequisites

This plan depends on two upstream contracts being in place before it can
type-check or run.

- **`src/types/index.ts` must export `BrainState` as a four-variant tagged
  union** with `status` membership exactly `"ok" | "no-brain-aide" |
  "no-mcp-entry" | "mcp-drift"`. The `"invalid-path"` variant (and any
  field references that imply `rootPath` validation, e.g. the
  `invalid-path` shape's `rootPath` carry) must be gone. `BrainToolResult`
  must remain typed as `{ status: BrainState["status"]; instructions:
  string }` — once `BrainState` shrinks, `BrainToolResult["status"]`
  inherits the four-state union automatically. Without this change, the
  removal of the `if (state.status === "invalid-path")` branch in this
  plan's step 2 produces a TS2367 (comparison appears unintentional) on
  the now-impossible discriminant value, and the new defensive-fallback
  return literal `{ status: "no-brain-aide", ... }` would be
  type-checked against a union that still includes `"invalid-path"`.

- **`src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts` must be refactored to never
  return `{ status: "invalid-path" }`.** The detector's pipeline must
  drop the `stat(config.rootPath)` step and the two return sites that
  emit `invalid-path`. After refactor, the function's return shape
  matches the new four-variant `BrainState`. Without this change, the
  type-system will still allow `state.status === "invalid-path"` to
  resolve as a defined case at runtime, even if the type union forbids
  it at compile time — and any code path in this tool that no longer
  handles it would route to the defensive fallback unintentionally.

- **`src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` already declares the four-state
  contract** (status: aligned, post-schema-collapse pivot). No spec
  change is required there; only its code refactor is the prerequisite.

These prerequisites are external to this plan's scope directory. The
orchestrator executes their plans (the type-shrink in `src/types/` and
the detector refactor in `src/service/buildBrainState/`) before this
plan's steps begin. This plan does NOT modify those files.

## Plan

### 1. Refactor `index.ts` to the four-state branch set

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/services/error-handling`

- [x] 1a. Delete the `INVALID_PATH_PROSE` constant declaration entirely
  (the string literal and its surrounding documentation comment, if the
  comment refers only to that constant). Leave the
  `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE`, `NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE`, and `MCP_DRIFT_PROSE`
  constants in place.

- [x] 1b. Update the `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE` constant's text. The path
  reference inside the prose currently reads `.aide/brain.aide`; change
  it to `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Every other word of the prose stays
  byte-identical — the load-bearing phrases (the npx command name, the
  `/aide:brain config` slash command pointer, the "do not proceed" clause)
  are unchanged. The exact target text:

  > "No `.aide/config/brain.aide` is present in this project. Tell the
  > user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init` to scaffold the brain config file,
  > or `/aide:brain config` if they want to be walked through it. Do
  > not proceed with this task as if the brain were available — the
  > file must exist before brain-dependent work continues."

  This is the only path-string change in the file. `NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE`
  and `MCP_DRIFT_PROSE` already reference `.aide/brain.aide`; per the
  spec context the path is the only mention to update for the
  `no-brain-aide` constant. (Audit `NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE` and
  `MCP_DRIFT_PROSE` while you are in the file — if either references a
  `brain.aide` path, update it to `.aide/config/brain.aide` for
  consistency. Each reference is the same load-bearing path-disclosure
  that `aide_init` writes, so they should match the new canonical
  location.)

- [x] 1c. Delete the `if (state.status === "invalid-path") return { ... }`
  branch entirely (the `if` head and the return statement). This branch
  is no longer reachable once the prerequisite type-shrink lands —
  TypeScript will surface the now-unreachable comparison as TS2367 if
  left in place. After deletion, the branch ladder is exactly three
  non-ok branches: `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`.

- [x] 1d. Retarget the defensive fallback (the final `return` after the
  `parseResult.kind === "ok"` check). The current fallback returns
  `{ status: "invalid-path", instructions: INVALID_PATH_PROSE }`; the
  new fallback returns `{ status: "no-brain-aide", instructions:
  NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE }`. The rationale is in the spec's Strategy
  paragraph "A defensive fallback guards the 'instructions never
  empty' invariant" — the upstream-contract-violation pattern this
  fallback exists for is "buildBrainState says ok but parser says
  missing/broken file," which is the missing-or-broken-file shape that
  `no-brain-aide` is the natural remediation for.

- [x] 1e. Update the orchestrator's JSDoc and the inline comment above
  the defensive-fallback `return` to reflect the new vocabulary. The
  JSDoc bullet that enumerated `invalid-path` should be removed; the
  inline comment above the defensive fallback should name
  `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE` (not `INVALID_PATH_PROSE`) as the fallback
  target and cite the same upstream-contract-violation reasoning. Do
  not enumerate the count of statuses inline — the spec deliberately
  avoids pinning a count, so the JSDoc should describe the contract
  in terms of the `BrainState` vocabulary rather than naming "four
  states."

- [x] 1f. Confirm the orchestrator's response literals never include a
  `name`, `backend`, or `connector` field. Each return statement should
  be exactly `{ status: <status>, instructions: <prose> }`. This is the
  no-discriminant-leakage invariant from the spec's outcomes.desired —
  any extra field re-introduces the registry-dispatch temptation on the
  consumer side. The current code already complies; verify nothing was
  reintroduced during the constant cleanup.

