## Decisions

- **The `INVALID_PATH_PROSE` constant deletes outright; no
  rename, no archive comment.** The schema collapse retired the
  `rootPath` field from `brain.aide`; the package no longer
  validates a path; there is no `invalid-path` `BrainState`
  variant; therefore there is no remediation surface for this
  prose to address. Keeping the constant as dead text invites
  future contributors to try wiring it back in. The spec's bad
  example explicitly forbids re-introducing both the branch and
  the constant.

- **Defensive fallback retargets to `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE`, not
  to `MCP_DRIFT_PROSE` or `NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE`.** The
  upstream-contract-violation pattern this fallback exists for
  is "`buildBrainState` reports `ok` but `parseBrainAide` says
  `missing` / `malformed-frontmatter` / `malformed-body`" — a
  missing-or-broken-file shape. `no-brain-aide` is the natural
  remediation for that shape (it tells the user to run init,
  which scaffolds the file). Routing to `mcp-drift` or
  `no-mcp-entry` would tell the user to run sync, which has
  nothing to read from if the file is missing or malformed.
  The spec's Strategy paragraph documents this reasoning
  verbatim; the choice is non-negotiable.

- **No `name`, no `backend`, no `connector` on the wire,
  enforced by an explicit test.** The spec's
  `outcomes.desired[0]` is unambiguous: the response carries
  `status` + `instructions` only. The new "no name, backend, or
  connector field" assertion (step 2h) is an invariant test —
  it does not test a behavior, it tests an absence. Adding any
  one of those fields back, even as a "harmless" diagnostic,
  re-creates the registry-dispatch temptation on the consumer
  side and should fail this test by construction.

- **The `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE` text-update is the only path-
  string change in the file.** Of the three remaining
  remediation prose constants, only `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE`
  carries the `.aide/brain.aide` path string in its body. (The
  audit step in 1b says to verify this; if `NO_MCP_ENTRY_PROSE`
  or `MCP_DRIFT_PROSE` carry the path string, update them too —
  they should all reflect the new canonical location.) The
  load-bearing phrases (the `npx aidemd-mcp` commands, the
  `/aide:brain config` shortcut, the "do not proceed" clauses)
  are unchanged; the test assertions for those phrases continue
  to hold.

- **Tests do not pin a status count.** The test file's existing
  header described "five-status branching"; the rewrite (step
  2a) describes the contract in terms of the `BrainState`
  vocabulary instead. This avoids the enumeration-drift pattern
  that the original spec drift exemplified — pinning a count
  guarantees the test header rots the next time the parent
  vocabulary changes. The test bodies enumerate the actual
  branches they cover (one describe block per branch), which is
  the registry-as-source-of-truth equivalent at the test layer.

- **Prerequisites are flagged, not absorbed.** The type-shrink
  (`src/types/index.ts`) and the detector refactor
  (`src/service/buildBrainState/`) are explicit prerequisites.
  This plan does NOT modify those files; doing so would expand
  the plan's scope past its module boundary and tangle three
  refactors into one. The orchestrator executes those plans
  first; this plan executes only after both have landed and
  type-checked clean.

- **No helper-folder extraction.** The orchestrator stays
  single-file. The four (now three) remediation prose constants
  and the four-branch ladder fit comfortably within the
  progressive-disclosure single-helper exception in the parent
  spec's Context section. A `composeInstructions/` helper
  folder is what was deleted in the prior plan; recreating it
  for three constants and three `if` branches would re-add
  ceremony without payoff.
